      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nLet $Y=\\frac{X}{10}-1.$ The distribution of $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $c$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $a$ | $b$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $d$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's add a $Y=\\frac{X}{10}-1$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, ${f}_{X}(x)={f}_{Y}(y)$ for corresponding values of $X$ and $Y.$ Therefore, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nSo, we have $a=\\frac{5}{12},b=\\frac{1}{12},c=1,$ and therefore $a+b+c=\\frac{3}{2}.$",
